PATCH 539: the AI aiming vectors at DGROUP:0x9684/0x9690 are DWORD triples, but the port declared all
six slots `undefined2` -- so every 32-bit world coordinate was truncated to its low 16 bits.

asm 1a265 (FUN_1000_a265, the target aiming-vector calc) writes them with the 0x66 operand-size
prefix, i.e. 32-bit stores:

   1a270: 66 8b 45 04    mov    0x4(%di),%eax     ; self X  (DWORD)
   1a274: 66 a3 84 96    mov    %eax,0x9684       ; DWORD store
   1a278: 66 8b 45 08    mov    0x8(%di),%eax     ; self Y
   1a27c: 66 a3 88 96    mov    %eax,0x9688
   1a284: 66 0f b7 87 .. movzwl -0x1a40(%bx),%eax ; per-type height LUT
   1a28a: 66 03 45 0c    add    0xc(%di),%eax     ; + self Z   (32-bit add)
   1a28e: 66 a3 8c 96    mov    %eax,0x968c
   1a292: 66 8b 44 04    mov    0x4(%si),%eax     ; target X
   1a296: 66 a3 90 96    mov    %eax,0x9690
   1a29a: 66 8b 44 08    mov    0x8(%si),%eax     ; target Y
   1a29e: 66 a3 94 96    mov    %eax,0x9694
   1a2c2: 66 03 44 0c    add    0xc(%si),%eax     ; target Z + LUT
   1a2c6: 66 a3 98 96    mov    %eax,0x9698
   1a2ca: be 84 96       mov    $0x9684,%si       ; -> a18e over the two vectors
   1a2cd: bf 90 96       mov    $0x9690,%di

The macros were `(*(undefined2 *)(g_mem+0x2568x))`.  Every one of the six assignments therefore stored
only the low word and left the high word holding whatever the previous occupant of those bytes was,
while the consumers (0731 bearing, 0927 range, 077e pitch) read all six back as 32-bit.

The angle solver itself is fine.  Measured with a reference atan2, FUN_0000_0731 returns 54998 where
the true bearing is 54997 -- one unit of fixed-point rounding.  It was simply being fed garbage:

  [a265] di=cf11 obj=(   570286,  1201009,  2048)  si=c34d tgt=(   549136,  1017675,     0)
         vec5684=(    45998,    21361,  4096)      vec5690=(    24848,    34635,  1792)

Consequence: every AI unit aimed along a bearing computed from truncated coordinates.  The turret slew
is not at fault -- word[di+0x10] tracks the computed aim word[di+0x9b] exactly; the computed aim was
simply wrong.  Measured aiming error at launch, AZER1:

  before:  used=26200 required=  856   (-139 deg)
           used=54998 required=33965   (-115 deg)
  after:   used=33966 required=33965   (+0.01 deg)

This is why projectiles never came within the collision bound: with patch 538's range test corrected,
the closest approach of any projectile to any object over 538010 tests was 11601 units against a bound
of 1280.  The projectiles were flying accurately along a bearing that pointed nowhere near the target.

The six slots are 4 bytes apart with no overlap (0x25684..0x2569b, and DAT_2000_569c begins exactly
where 0x25698's dword ends), so widening the macros is exact.  Every existing user already treats them
as wide -- a265 and ace0 assign 32-bit expressions to them, and the 1adea/1ae0d and 67221-67267 sites
read and add them as `long`.

AZER1 after: a296 16 -> 15 (a real kill), a294 105.

